%[text] # WD Lab: Signal Data Analysis & App Development
%[text] **Hands-on Lab | CSV → Import Data → Create Plot → Live Tasks → Signal Analyzer → App**
%[text] This lab is completed click by click. Every step uses a MATLAB Live Task or app, and you review the MATLAB code that is generated for you automatically. No code needs to be typed by hand.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Lab steps"}
%[text] **How to use this lab**
%[text] - Press **Run Section** one section at a time (do not use Run All)
%[text] - ![](text:image:5c2d)
%[text] - A code line starting with `% ▶` marks where a Live Task goes: click that line → **Live Editor** tab → **Task** → choose the task name
%[text] - Alternatively, type the task name (for example `Clean Missing Data`) on a code line and pick it from the suggestion list
%[text] - Every task has a **▾** button at the bottom right; use **Show code** to see the generated code \
%%
%[text] ## 0. Get ready
%[text] Set the **Current Folder** to the folder that contains `WD_Lab_Signal_Data.csv` and `WDSignalExplorer.mlapp`, then run this section. Both file names must appear below.
ls WD_Lab_Signal_Data.csv %[output:09140209]
ls WDSignalExplorer.mlapp %[output:99d4a485]
%%
%[text] ## 1. Import Data — bring the CSV into MATLAB
%[text] **Click:** insert the **Import Data** task → select the file `WD_Lab_Signal_Data.csv` → set **Output Type** to **Column vectors** → press **Import**
%[text] **What you should see:** the variables `Time_s`, `Reference_um` and `Evaluation_um` appear in the Workspace (4096 rows; empty cells in the file become `NaN`)
%[text] ![](text:image:4f05)
% ▶ Insert the "Import Data" Live Task on this line
%%
%[text] ## 2. Create Plot — look at the data before changing anything
%[text] **Click:** insert the **Create Plot** task → select the data `Evaluation_um` → set the X axis to `Time_s` → choose the **plot** (line) chart
%[text] **Observe:** the line breaks in places (missing values), there are sharp vertical spikes (outliers), and the whole trace slowly rises (drift). Steps 3–5 deal with these three problems.
%[text] ![](text:image:4fcc)
% ▶ Insert the "Create Plot" Live Task on this line
%%
%[text] ## 3. Clean Missing Data — fill the gaps
%[text] **Click:** insert the **Clean Missing Data** task → Input data: `Evaluation_um` → X-axis: `Time_s` → Cleaning method: **Fill missing** → Fill method: **Linear interpolation** → click the output name and rename it to `filledData`
%[text] **What you should see:** the task reports 24 missing entries → after filling, the plot has no gaps and the number of rows is unchanged
% ▶ Insert the "Clean Missing Data" Live Task on this line
%%
%[text] ## 4. Clean Outlier Data — remove the spikes
%[text] **Click:** insert the **Clean Outlier Data** task → Input data: `filledData` → Cleaning method: **Fill outliers** / **Linear interpolation** → Detection method: **Moving median** → Window: **101** → rename the output to `noOutlierData`
%[text] **Try it:** change the Threshold factor or the Window and watch how the marked points change
% ▶ Insert the "Clean Outlier Data" Live Task on this line
%%
%[text] ## 5. Find and Remove Trends — remove the drift
%[text] **Click:** insert the **Find and Remove Trends** task → Input data: `noOutlierData` → Trend type: **Linear** → rename the output to `detrendedData`
%[text] **What you should see:** the Trend plot is a straight rising line and the Detrended plot is centered around zero
% ▶ Insert the "Find and Remove Trends" Live Task on this line
%%
%[text] ## 6. Smooth Data — reduce noise (experiment)
%[text] **Click:** insert the **Smooth Data** task → Input data: `detrendedData` → Smoothing method: **Savitzky-Golay** → Window: **11** → rename the output to `smoothedData`
%[text] **Try it:** change the Window to 31 and check whether the peaks become smaller. Step 8 uses Signal Analyzer to decide which value to keep.
% ▶ Insert the "Smooth Data" Live Task on this line
%%
%[text] ## 7. Before and after
%[text] Run this section to see how the four Live Tasks changed the data.
tiledlayout(2,1)
nexttile
plot(Time_s,Evaluation_um)
grid on; ylabel("um"); title("Before: raw CSV data")
nexttile
plot(Time_s,detrendedData,Time_s,smoothedData)
grid on; xlabel("Time (s)"); ylabel("um"); title("After: Live Tasks")
legend("detrendedData","smoothedData")
%%
%[text] ## 8. Signal Analyzer — time and frequency domains
%[text] Run this section. MATLAB combines the signals into a timetable and opens **Signal Analyzer** automatically.
signals = timetable(seconds(Time_s),Reference_um,detrendedData,smoothedData, ...
    VariableNames=["Reference","EvaluationClean","EvaluationSmoothed"]);
signalAnalyzer(signals)
%[text] **In the app:**
%[text] 1. Drag all three signals into the same Display and zoom into the same time range
%[text] 2. Press **Spectrum** to add the frequency view. Look for the peaks near **120 Hz** and **250 Hz**, and compare Reference with EvaluationClean
%[text] 3. Compare EvaluationClean with EvaluationSmoothed in the Spectrum view: does the 250 Hz peak change?
%[text] 4. Select one signal → **Duplicate** → **Preprocess** (for example Lowpass) → press **Generate Function** to see the code the app creates \
%%
%[text] ## 9. Filter Designer — specify a filter in the app (optional)
%[text] Run this section to open **Filter Designer**, then try: Response type **Lowpass** → Fs = **2000** Hz → Fpass = **300**, Fstop = **400** → **Design Filter** → view the Magnitude and Phase Response → menu **File → Generate MATLAB Code**
filterDesigner
%%
%[text] ## 10. App Development — use the result in WD Signal Explorer
%[text] Run this section to save the prepared result as a new CSV and open the example app built with App Designer.
preparedData = table(Time_s,Reference_um,detrendedData, ...
    VariableNames=["Time_s","Reference_um","EvaluationClean_um"]);
writetable(preparedData,"WD_Lab_Prepared_Signal.csv")
WDSignalExplorer
%[text] **In the app:**
%[text] 1. Press **Load CSV** → select `WD_Lab_Prepared_Signal.csv`
%[text] 2. Time column = `Time_s`, Signal column = `EvaluationClean_um`
%[text] 3. Switch between **Time domain / Frequency domain** and read the RMS and Peak-to-peak values
%[text] 4. Change the Signal column to `Reference_um` to compare
%[text] 5. Press **Export processed CSV** and give the file a new name \
%[text] **Look at the app structure:** open `WDSignalExplorer.mlapp` in App Designer → **Code View** → notice that the Load CSV button calls `readtable`, and the calculations live in the methods `processSignal`, `updatePlot` and `updateMetrics`. The code generated by the Live Tasks and Signal Analyzer today can be placed there.
%%
%[text] ## 11. Save your work
%[text] **File → Save As** a `.mlx` file with your name, so that all Live Task settings and the generated code are kept.
%[text] **What you can do after this lab:**
%[text] - Import a CSV into MATLAB and plot it without writing code
%[text] - Fill missing values, remove spikes, remove drift and smooth a signal with Live Tasks, with reusable generated code
%[text] - Compare signals in the time and frequency domains with Signal Analyzer
%[text] - Hand the prepared result to an app built with App Designer \
%[text] References: [Live Editor Tasks](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/live-editor-tasks.html) · [Signal Analyzer](https://www.mathworks.com/help/signal/ref/signalanalyzer-app.html) · [Filter Designer](https://www.mathworks.com/help/signal/ref/filterdesigner-app.html) · [App Designer](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/app-designer.html)

